<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salim Rubaya Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1935–1978</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Salem Rubaya Ali, Salmin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salim Rubaya Ali was a Yemeni revolutionary who headed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>'s Presidential Council from 1969 to 1978 after the Corrective Move. A leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a>, he leaned towards Maoist and more independent policies and sought better relations with Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, clashing with the pro-Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-fattah-ismail">Abdul Fattah Ismail</a>. In June 1978 he was overthrown and executed after a power struggle linked to the killing of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>'s president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salim-rubaya-ali.jpg" alt="Salim Rubaya Ali" width="231" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_portrait_of_Salim_Rubaya_Ali.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
